Sheets: opening a workbook with conditional formats no longer evaluates every condition twice over.

The optimal row height pass at the end of loading (ScDocShell::UpdateAllRowHeights ->
ScColumn::GetNeededSize) asked every cell for its conditional style (ScDocument::GetCondResult).
A condition with relative references is evaluated through a temporary formula cell per cell, and
that cell compiled the condition's RPN again every time. On a synthetic 5 MB workbook (31 000 rows,
4000 cell formats, an expression rule over 15 columns) the load took 42 s on Linux (4 CPUs); 19 s
without the expression rule and 11 s without any conditional format. The 10 MB version took 60 s on
a 4-core Windows PC, 35 s of them "Not responding".

- ScConditionEntry compiles the RPN of a relative condition once (mpRelCode1/2, a copy that shares
  the code tokens with pFormula1/2, dropped wherever the cached formula cells are dropped) and the
  temporary cells reuse it. Only formulas whose RPN cannot depend on the position use it (single
  references, values, operators, functions, jumps); ranges, names, labels, table, external and DDE
  references keep the per-cell compilation.
- GetNeededSize evaluates the conditions only when one of the cell's conditional formats may give it
  a style with items that change the size; a style (with its parents) that only sets font colour,
  background, borders or shadow cannot, so its condition is not evaluated for the size.
The resulting row heights and conditional styles are unchanged.

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).
Test: CppunitTest_sc_ucalc_condformat testCondFormatRelativeExpressionCompiledOnce (a relative
condition evaluated at many positions and after its input changed, and a range condition);
the existing conditional format and import tests; tests/unit/test_large_office_open_patches.py.
